- One reader: injectDebriefCards, field.js line 2669 ("const _hit = await _dc.match(_debriefCacheKey);").
  On a hit it uses the payload and never asks the relay. It does not check the
  entry's age.
- One writer: injectDebriefCards, field.js line 2683, after a relay fetch on a miss.
- One evictor: the sw.js activate handler ("evict field-debriefs entries older
  than 7 days"). It deletes an entry only when X-Cache-Time is more than 7 days old.
- index.html lines 7726-7744 are the pre-commit hook's copy of field.js.
  Nothing else opens the cache: 3 of the 6 caches.open calls in field.js and
  sw.js name it (field.js lines 2669 and 2683, sw.js line 43); the other 3 are
  sw.js's SHELL_CACHE and API_CACHE.
  (Corrected after review: the first draft said 2 of 6.)

### T0.2 every other client store of relay archive payloads, with its eviction

Command: grep -n "caches.open\|localStorage.setItem\|sessionStorage.setItem\|indexedDB.open" src/legacy/field.js sw.js
The CC-CMD's grep named only caches.open and localStorage. sessionStorage and
IndexedDB were added because the first archive reader found
(fetchSeriesArchive) writes sessionStorage.

Archive reads: every fetch of /archive/* or /context/* in field.js (19 call sites:
17 with the route on the fetch( line, 2 with it on the next line, the
bracket-replay and last-meeting reads), and what each one persists:

| reader | route | stored in | read-time age check |
|---|---|---|---|
| injectDebriefCards | /context/game/{id} | Cache API field-debriefs (persists across reloads) | **none** |
| hydrateMissedRecaps | /context/game/{id} | nothing; and it reads `data.briefs`, a key the body never has, so it never finds a recap (filed: docs/CC-CMD-2026-10-20-missed-recap-reads-a-shape-the-relay-never-sends.md) | n/a |
| brief-quality health panel | /archive/query | sessionStorage | 30 min |
| archive timeline | /archive/query | sessionStorage | 30 min |
| upsets | /archive/upsets, /archive/query | sessionStorage | 30 min |
| journalism archive | /archive/query | sessionStorage | 30 min |
| broadcast archaeology | /archive/date/{iso} | sessionStorage | 30 min |
| fetchSeriesArchive | /archive/series/{key} | sessionStorage | 30 min |
| fetchLastMeeting, fetchArchiveDate, drama-missing, bracket-replay | /archive/* (GET) | nothing | n/a |
| archive writers: /archive/brief x2, /archive/game x1, /archive/drama x3 | POST | n/a | n/a |

  19 = 13 readers + 6 writers. (Corrected after review: the first draft also
  listed bracket-replay as a writer; it is a GET reader only.)

- localStorage: 57 setItem calls in field.js (59 matching lines, 2 of them the
  `// Usage: localStorage.setItem('FIELD_DEBUG','1')` comments). 13 sit in a function that talks
  to the relay. None of those 13 stores an /archive or /context payload. They
  hold BDL player data (24 h and 4 h TTL), FIFA rank (TTL), the playoff leaders
  (TTL), journalism scout picks, live soccer stats (date-keyed), and the
  client's own finals list (date-keyed).
- IndexedDB:
  - field-snapshots holds `main.innerHTML`, rendered debriefs included,
    saved on visibilitychange/beforeunload and painted at boot when under
    6 h old. The first renderAll replaces it, and that render's debriefs go
    through injectDebriefCards, so a stale debrief in a snapshot lasts until
    the first render of the next load;
  - field_prefs (sw.js) holds preferences.
- sw.js fetch handler: no relay host in isAPI, so no relay response is cached by
  the service worker (API_CACHE holds espn, open-meteo, sportsdb, the-odds, fpl,
  statsapi only).

Result: field-debriefs is the only store of a relay archive payload that
outlives the browser session and has no read-time age check. Every sessionStorage
copy dies with the tab and expires after 30 minutes.

### T0.3 does /context/game/{id} carry a change signal?

Relay route source (field-relay-nba src/index.js at 7829082c, the /context/game handler):
  const cacheKey = `ctx:${id}`
  KV hit:   headers 'Cache-Control': 'public,max-age=60', 'X-Cache': 'HIT'
  fresh:    headers 'Cache-Control': `public,max-age=${isFinal ? 300 : 60}`
  KV write: expirationTtl: isFinal ? 300 : 60
No ETag, no Last-Modified, no digest header.
(Corrected after review: the first draft quoted max-age=60 for the fresh path;
for a final it is 300. A revalidation within 300 s of the previous fetch can be
answered from the browser's HTTP cache.)

A failed D1 sub-query does not produce a non-2xx. findGame and the four
fan-out lookups are settled; a rejection becomes null for that section and an
entry in `_errors`, and the body is still `ok: true`, HTTP 200, KV-cached
(300 s for a final). `_errors` is present only when non-empty. Found by the
review, not by this TASK 0's first pass, and it decides what a revalidation may
accept (§0.4). The relay's caching of such a body is filed separately:
field-relay-nba docs/CC-CMD-2026-10-20-context-game-caches-partial-answers.md.

Probe (mcp FIELD_Handoff probe_relay_route, relay self-fetch), 2026-10-20:
  ~16:00Z  GET /context/game/espn:401866408 -> 200 application/json, 3105 bytes
  ~16:00Z  GET /context/game/espn:401866408 -> 200 application/json, 3105 bytes
  16:33:58Z GET /context/game/espn:401866408 -> 200 application/json, 3105 bytes
  bodies byte-identical: yes, all three (the third compared with node against
  the first, saved; the first is outbox/fixtures/context-game-espn-401866408-2026-10-20.json
  `repaired`, whose JSON.stringify is the same 3105 bytes)
  The two ~16:00Z reads cannot refute determinism: the second is a KV hit of
  the first (review finding). The 16:34Z read is 34 minutes later, past the
  300 s KV TTL, so it was computed again unless another reader refreshed
  `ctx:espn:401866408` in between. The self-probe does not return X-Cache, so
  that cannot be excluded; it is the remaining limit of this check.
  top-level keys: ok, id, resolved, id_form, game, archive, series, enrichment, bracketDelta
  game.* time fields: created_at "2026-08-29 10:01:28", finalized_at "2026-08-30 02:00:38",
    start_time "2026-08-29T22:30Z". None changed when the odds were repaired
    on 2026-10-20 (closing_odds_parsed.moneyline now {home:-345, away:275}).
  No field is computed per request: no generated_at, no now, no request id.

Result:
- The body carries no field that changes when the archive row changes. The
  client cannot cheaply tell a corrected row from an unchanged one: it has to
  fetch the body and compare it.
- No field is computed per request: findGame, findBriefs, findSeries,
  findEnrichment and findBracketDelta read D1 only (no Date.now, random value
  or request id). The body therefore changes when the game row, its joined
  briefs/series/enrichment/bracket rows change, or when a D1 sub-query fails
  (`_errors`). The first two are corrections; the third is not, and a
  revalidation must refuse it.
- The relay's own copies clear within 300 s for a final (KV) plus 60 s
  (browser HTTP cache).

## §0.4 TASK 1 decision (owner-delegated: "next approved", 2026-10-20)

Chosen: (b) revalidate on read, with three rules the review added:
- a non-2xx, a network failure, or a 200 carrying `_errors` is not a
  correction: the cached copy and its render stay, and debrief:revalidate is
  recorded. A body with `_errors` is never written to the Cache API, on a miss
  either.
- an unchanged body writes nothing, so X-Cache-Time keeps dating the copy for
  sw.js's 7-day eviction (the first version rewrote it on every load).
- a changed body re-runs the injection for that game's cards only. The first
  version re-ran the whole pass, which re-requested every card still waiting
  on its own first answer (K corrected games x M pending cards).
- (a) an age check cannot tell a corrected row from an unchanged one (T0.3).
  Whatever N is chosen, a correction stays invisible for up to N hours.
- (c) fixes the entries from this incident only. The CC-CMD says so itself.
- (b) bounds a correction at the next page load. It keeps Gap 12's offline
  render: a failed revalidation leaves the cached render in place. It follows
  sw.js's own staleWhileRevalidate, the shell cache's pattern.
- Cost: one /context/game request per cached final card per page load,
  issued alongside the cached render. Within 300 s of the previous fetch the
  browser's HTTP cache may answer it. Otherwise the relay answers from KV
  (`ctx:{id}`, 300 s for a final), or computes the body: 7 D1 statements for
  espn:401866408 (findGame 4, findBriefs 1, findSeries 1, findBracketDelta 1;
  findEnrichment returns before querying with no date in the id), read from the
  branches at relay 7829082c; a game with a series_key adds 2, plus one KV get
  and one put. Within a load, the in-memory
  Map serves every later renderAll.

